 </div>
 <div class="dtexp-foot"><span>Alegra · Confidential · table = durable semester Δ · chart = monthly flow · Pricing incl. discounts</span><span>Expansion · 1/3</span></div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 2 -->
<div class="dtexp-board-slide">
 <div class="dtexp-head"><h2>The driver of net upsell is the customer's income — not us</h2><span class="dtexp-per">Discussion topic · 2/3</span></div>
 <div class="dtexp-body">
  <div class="dtexp-eye">What moves net upsell · plan changes vs. customer income (FX-neutral)</div>
  <div class="dtexp-two">
    <div id="dtexp-chart2"></div>
    <div class="dtexp-stat">
      <div class="dtexp-big">~6 in 10<span> of upsell reverses</span></div>
      <p style="margin:9px 0 0">The scatter shows net upsell <b>rising in high-income months and falling in low-income ones</b> — plan changes track the customer's income, which is seasonal (peaks in <b>December</b>, drops in <b>January</b>).</p>
      <p>So the same accounts bounce up and back down: <b>most upsell comes from accounts that also downgrade later</b> (chronic oscillators). It's oscillation on the customer's income cycle — not durable expansion.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="dtexp-take"><b>Net upsell is a real, deliberate customer decision — but it's driven by the customer's own income, which is seasonal and outside our control.</b> That's why it doesn't compound into structural expansion; it just rides the customer's revenue up and back down.</div>
 </div>
 <div class="dtexp-foot"><span>Alegra · Confidential · fact_customers_mrr (plan moves) × income_by_logo (FX-neutralized)</span><span>Expansion · 2/3</span></div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 3 -->
<div class="dtexp-board-slide">
 <div class="dtexp-head"><h2>What the 3-ICP model attacks</h2><span class="dtexp-per">Discussion topic · 3/3 · the strategy</span></div>
 <div class="dtexp-body">
  <div class="dtexp-eye">The strategy · turning expansion into a lever we control</div>
  <p class="dtexp-lead">Each weak spot in today's expansion maps to a design choice in the 3-ICP model — expansion tied to <b>value and usage</b>, not the customer's income cycle.</p>
  <div class="dtexp-cards">
    <div class="dtexp-card"><div class="dtexp-t"><span class="dtexp-num">1</span>Today's upsell is seasonal — not ours</div><div class="dtexp-d">Plan changes just follow the customer's own income — they upgrade in December's peak and downgrade in January. It's their business cycle, which we don't control, and it nets to zero over a year.</div><span class="dtexp-tag">net upsell → negative in 1H26</span></div>
    <div class="dtexp-card"><div class="dtexp-t"><span class="dtexp-num">2</span>Move upsell to real value</div><div class="dtexp-d">Tie upgrades to usage the customer controls and we can meter: <b>more users, warehouses, API, storage, inventory items, AI</b>. Then upsell becomes a lever <b>we</b> drive — structural expansion, not the income cycle.</div><span class="dtexp-tag" style="color:#2f9e78;background:#e7f6f0">usage-based · steerable</span></div>
    <div class="dtexp-card"><div class="dtexp-t"><span class="dtexp-num">3</span>Clearer initial offer + cross-sell bundles</div><div class="dtexp-d">Two things to fix together: the <b>over-bundled initial sale unwinds</b> (−1%/sem), and genuine cross-sell is still modest (+1%/sem, room to grow). The 3-ICP model makes the entry offer <b>clearer per profile</b> — right-sized first, then <b>attractive cross-product bundles</b> to grow the second product.</div><span class="dtexp-tag">cross +1% · initial unwind −1%</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dtexp-take"><b>The goal: move expansion from passive (income cycle + a fading tariff bump) to structural (value + usage the customer keeps).</b> Separately, the biggest leak is still churn — the retention agenda (annual billing, onboarding, accountant multi-logo lock), distinct from the expansion levers above.</div>
 </div>
 <div class="dtexp-foot"><span>Alegra · Confidential · pricing strategy — expansion</span><span>Expansion · 3/3</span></div>
</div>

